SUMMARY:Santa Rosa Taco Tuesday Bike Ride
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T000000
UID:260504W_qzQ-lm9kejD57T3inNmg
DESCRIPTION:A fun community bike ride through the heart of Santa Rosa. Com
 e join us this Tuesday! We roll through the JC and McDonald Ave neighborho
 od\, stop in Courthouse Square\, then on to the taco trucks at Mitote Food
  Park in Roseland for dinner! We depart Mitote at 8:15pm\, ride back throu
 gh Courthouse Square\, then on to Yogurt Farms for dessert. This ride is 8
  miles round trip and rides all year long. Rides canceled for rain and hol
 idays. All riders welcome. Bring the whole family! Meet at SRJC parking lo
 t at 550 Silva Ave. We are renting the use of this parking lot every Tuesd
 ay from 5:30pm to 9:30 pm. Taco Tuesday riders can park in the lot during 
 that time frame for free. Please park cars along the back wall on the east
  side. And stage in the row on the northern side of the lot \\(closest to 
 Silva Ave\\). Meet at 5:30pm\; pedals up at 6pm.
LOCATION:SRJC Parking Lot
URL:https://www.visitsantarosa.com/events/#jlupcoming-events
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Kabbalah of Meaning
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T193000
UID:261001cBi6UhPpc06NmO4CwOWIJg
DESCRIPTION:Jewish Wisdom for a Life That Matters\n\nNo matter how much we
  achieve\, something within us still asks: “Is this it? Isn’t there so
 mething more?”\n\nThe Kabbalah of Meaning is a new six-session JLI cours
 e exploring the Jewish answer to that question. What are we really searchi
 ng for—and where can it be found?\n\nDrawing on Kabbalah and centuries o
 f Jewish wisdom\, we’ll uncover the essential building blocks of a deepl
 y meaningful life. We’ll explore how purpose can be found in our work\, 
 relationships\, challenges\, accomplishments\, and even the ordinary routi
 nes that fill our days.\n\nYou don’t have to revamp your life. You don
 ’t need to meditate on a mountaintop. You simply need a new lens through
  which to see the rich world of meaning already woven into every part of y
 our life.\n\nThoughtful\, practical\, and surprisingly relevant\, this cou
 rse offers fresh perspectives and useful tools for living with greater cla
 rity\, connection\, and intention.\n\nTry 1st class free.\n\nNo previous J
 ewish learning required. Come as you are—and bring your questions.
LOCATION:Chabad Jewish Center of Petaluma
URL:https://www.jewishpetaluma.com/templates/articlecco_cdo/aid/7522546/je
 wish/The-Kabbalah-of-Meaning.htm
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dia de Muertos at Jack London State Historic Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T170000
UID:260930WcKtWxqXhUq9EuktiF1wiQ
DESCRIPTION:In recognition of Día de Muertos\, Jack London State Historic
  Park will present two ofrendas in the House of Happy Walls Museum from Oc
 t. 4 to Nov. 6\, 2026.\n\nDia de Muertos\, or Day of the Dead\, is a tradi
 tion that began in Mexican and other Latino cultures.  An ofrenda\, Spani
 sh for ‘offering\,’ is an altar created to honor and welcome the memor
 y of those who have died.\n\nThe Legacy Ofrenda will honor writer and adve
 nturer Jack London\, Charmian London\, and other individuals connected to 
 the history of the property. Through photographs\, reproductions\, traditi
 onal elements of an ofrenda\, and carefully selected symbolic objects\, th
 e installation will reflect Jack and Charmian's partnership\, writing and 
 travels\, their life at Beauty Ranch\, their connection to the land\, and 
 the legacies they left behind.\n\nThe Community Ofrenda will be a shared\,
  participatory offering. Visitors\, locals\, volunteers\, Board members\, 
 and employees can contribute a small photograph\, written name\, memory\, 
 drawing or message honoring someone who has passed away – including a be
 loved pet. \n\nThe installation will grow throughout October as more peop
 le participate.  Materials will be available at the display for visitors 
 who would like to join the celebration and contribute a name\, memory or e
 ven a drawing of their loved one.
LOCATION:Jack London State Historic Park
URL:https://jacklondonpark.com/dia-de-muertos-2026/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Independent Projects with Amy Hamley
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T210000
UID:260926-gq-OSUz9E2B4Ds7-JCH3g
DESCRIPTION:Every Tuesday · 6 Weeks\n\nDate & Time\n\nOctober 6 – Novem
 ber 10th\, 2026\n\n6:00 PM – 9:00 PM\n\nLocation\n\nRoom 117\, Ceramics 
 Studio\n\nCategory\n\nCeramics\, Extended Classes\n\nExperience Level\n\nI
 ntermediate\n\nAges\n\n17\\+\n\nRegistration Fee:\n\n$200\n\nMaterials Fee
 :\n\n$30\n\nTotal Registration Cost:\n\n$230\n\nThis is a 6 week session w
 ith instructor\, Amy Hamley. This course is for students interested in exp
 loring both wheel throwing AND handbuilding\, and the combination of both 
 in one class! Learn from Hamley’s years of expertise in the field and en
 joy creating handbuilt\, thrown\, and altered forms. Amy caters each sylla
 bus closely to her group of students\, ensuring that every project fits th
 e desires of participants. This course encourages folks to work on indepen
 dent projects in the class\, receiving one on one support from Amy through
 out the time. This course will include demos\, exercises\, and utilize a v
 ariety of methods to achieve the desired goal for each student. Some proje
 cts may include wheel thrown vases with altered adornments\, best practice
 s for slump and hump molds\, and more.
LOCATION:Sonoma Community Center
URL:https://sonomacommunitycenter.org/programs/independent-projects-august
 /
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Antarctica: The Frozen Desert | Photographs by Vegar Abelsnes
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T170000
UID:260914TBex2BcnWkSYJbXGV2l5qA
DESCRIPTION:1st Street Gallery at WTRSHD will present “Antarctica: The F
 rozen Desert\,” a new body of fine art photography by Vegar Abelsnes\, o
 pening September 26th\, 2026\, and on view September through November.  T
 he exhibition marks a deliberate turn in Abelsnes’ photography\; after m
 ore than two decades of working in a commercial and advertising capacity\,
  he has embarked on a career as a photo and field guide in the polar regio
 ns. The exhibit features work generated over 6 voyages to the Antarctic Pe
 ninsula. He is returning to Antarctica in January and will add the Falklan
 ds and South Georgia to his list of destinations to guide and photograph.\
 n\nTraveling to some of the most remote and inhospitable coastlines on Ear
 th\, Abelsnes has photographed a landscape that exists almost entirely out
 side of human time. The ice in these images began forming millions of year
 s ago\, while Homo sapiens have only existed for roughly 300\,000 years. D
 efined by such a scale\, Antarctica is less a place than a record — vast
 \, indifferent\, treacherous\, and staggeringly beautiful\, holding a kind
  of cosmic truth that Abelsnes says is more important now than ever before
 .
LOCATION:1st Street Gallery @ WTRSHD
URL:https://vegarabelsnes.com/ANTARCTICA:-The-Frozen-Desert/thumbs
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Formative Influence Art Exhibit
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T160000
UID:260909ScCb4hSrq0mURiuYhjjmPA
DESCRIPTION:Formative Influence explores the transformative power of light
  across five bodies of work. Inspired by the landscapes of Northern Califo
 rnia and the American West\, the exhibition examines the shift from photog
 raphy\, where light is observed and captured\, to painting\, where light i
 s created to shape new visual experiences. Rather than documenting the lan
 dscape\, these works evoke its emotional resonance\, emphasizing atmospher
 e\, memory\, and perception over literal representation.
LOCATION:Marin Center
URL:https://www.marincultural.org/exhibitions
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:California Native Creations Art Show
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T163000
UID:260909Pat66ukLK0G6JtJqTN0X_g
DESCRIPTION:September is the 17th Annual Pomo Honoring Month. On Monday\, 
 Sept. 14\, the 6th California Native Creations Art Show opens Person Senio
 r Wing\, Finley Community Center\, 2060 W. College Ave.\, Santa Rosa\, CA.
  The show is free and open to the public Monday – Friday\, 8:30 a.m.-4:3
 0 p.m.\, through November 19.\n\nThe established and up-and-coming native 
 artists create with digital media\, paint and traditional materials in the
 ir art\, weaving\, regalia\, beadwork\, jewelry\, baskets and quilts. The
  artists include:\n\n·         Meyo Marrufo\, painter\, guest cur
 ator for the de Young Museum and the Sonoma Valley Art Museum\,\n\n·  
        Buffie Campbell\, Vice Chair\, Sherwood Valley Rancheria\, cu
 ltural teacher and master basket maker\,\n\n·         Henry Edwar
 d Frank\, Yurok/Kashaya Pomo\, who will show paintings and work with Legos
  titled Mind Prison\,\n\n·         Digital artist Nicolas \\(Nico
 \\) Vargas\, Kashia Band of Pomo Indians\, whose art was featured at the 5
 5th Annual Stanford Powwow.\n\n·         plus Gloria Carretero\; 
 Rose Hammock\; Rudy Hammock\; Jocelyn Garcia\; Arella Ortega LaRose\, Kash
 ia Band of Pomo Indians\; Angelica Smith\, Manchester Point Arena and Yoka
 yo Rancheria\; Maria Pasalo\; Michelle Napoli\; Martina Morgan\, Kashia Ba
 nd of Pomo Indians\; Hands Lopez\; Shashoni Hanover-Redenbaugh\; Katie Wi
 lliams-Elliott\, Juan Partida\, and Mia Pasalo\, Big Valley Pomo Tribe.
LOCATION:Person Senior Wing\, Finley Community Center
URL:http://www.pomoproject.org/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
